{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794589904657,"sessionID":"ses_e2a1c240fffexkkiB53DGo0HVn","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5e4cd0f001A1dfG0KGoSdytf","sessionID":"ses_e2a1c240fffexkkiB53DGo0HVn","messageID":"msg_1d5e4ac600018bPnAmX40BcPiU","type":"tool","id":"call_d22ffc7734d742cf96304516","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md","limit":80},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md, lines 1-80\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-create-readme\n3: description: Author sk-doc folder, code-folder and skill/project READMEs from local evidence.\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 1.3.16.0\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # Create README (workflow)\n9: \n10: `create-readme` is the README authoring packet in the `sk-doc` family. It writes current-state folder READMEs from local evidence, using packet-local templates under `assets/` and shared create-quality-control validators under `../shared`.\n11: \n12: ---\n13: \n14: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n15: \n16: ### Activation Triggers\n17: \n18: Use this packet when the request involves:\n19: \n20: - Creating or refreshing a folder `README.md`.\n21: - Documenting a project, skill, feature, component or source-code directory.\n22: - Writing developer orientation for a code folder, including topology, boundaries, entrypoints and validation.\n23: - Running `/create:readme`.\n24: \n25: Keyword triggers: `create readme`, `/create:readme`, `readme`, `folder readme`, `write readme`, `README.md`, `front-page overview`, `code folder readme`.\n26: \n27: ### When NOT to Use\n28: \n29: Use another `sk-doc` packet when:\n30: \n31: - The user wants to audit, validate, score, or optimize an existing README or another existing document without authoring or refreshing it. Use `create-quality-control`.\n32: - The user wants to scaffold a skill, agent, command, benchmark package, feature catalog, testing playbook, flowchart, or changelog. Use `create-skill`, `create-agent`, `create-command`, `create-benchmark`, `create-feature-catalog`, `create-manual-testing-playbook`, `create-flowchart`, or `create-changelog`.\n33: - The target is not markdown.\n34: - The folder is self-explanatory and a parent README or inline comments already give enough orientation.\n35: \n36: This is a nested workflow packet under the `sk-doc` parent hub. It owns README authoring only. It does not carry `graph-metadata.json`; advisor identity, skill graph metadata and cross-packet routing live at the `sk-doc` hub root.\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n41: \n42: `sk-doc` routes README authoring here: the request names a README, a folder readme, a code-folder readme or `/create:readme`.\n43: The hub's mode signal selects this packet.\n44: Three artifacts route here: a general README, a code-folder README and a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n45: The artifact decides the template before the folder decides the sections.\n46: \n47: Authoring starts from the matching template: `assets/readme-template.md` for a general README, `assets/readme-code-template.md` for a source-code folder, and `assets/architecture-template.md` for a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n48: `references/README.md` is the fallback route map when the artifact type or folder purpose is unclear.\n49: \n50: When nothing matches, report `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with the router's disambiguation checklist instead of guessing.\n51: \n52: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n53: \n54: ---\n55: \n56: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n57: \n58: Every run follows one operating model, whatever the artifact:\n59: \n60: 1. Read local evidence first: the target folder, nearby docs, package files, config files and existing commands. Never document unconfirmed files, commands, APIs or metrics.\n61: 2. Route by artifact type and folder purpose (`ROUTER.md` Section 3) to pick the output and template.\n62: 3. Draft current-state content only, in the smallest useful shape: a general README (Section 5) or a code-folder README (Section 6).\n63: 4. Copy the matching template as a scaffold, remove unused sections and put orientation first.\n64: 5. Validate the authored markdown and resolve local links before delivery (Section 7).\n65: \n66: Both README shapes share this lifecycle. The sections below give the detailed steps for each.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 4. README AUTHORING WORKFLOW\n71: \n72: 1. Identify the target folder, audience and README type before drafting.\n73: 2. Read the target folder contents, nearby documentation, package files, config files and existing commands.\n74: 3. Do not invent commands, files, APIs, metrics, features or capabilities.\n75: 4. Choose the smallest useful README shape. Use a general README for project, skill, feature and component roots. Use a code-folder README for source-directory orientation.\n76: 5. Write current-state documentation only. Use stable file paths, commands, feature names and source links instead of packet IDs, phase IDs, migration history or temporary planning labels.\n77: 6. Put orientation first: what this is, who it is for and how to use or navigate it.\n78: 7. Apply progressive disclosure: title and tagline answer \"what is this\", overview helps the reader decide relevance, quick start appears only when there is a runnable path and detailed reference comes later.\n79: 8. Use two-tier voice for project, skill and feature READMEs: plain narrative for overview and usage, precise reference tables for options, configuration and tool signatures.\n80: 9. Use technical reference voice for component and code-folder READMEs.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 81]","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":true},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md, lines 1-80\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-create-readme\n3: description: Author sk-doc folder, code-folder and skill/project READMEs from local evidence.\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 1.3.16.0\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # Create README (workflow)\n9: \n10: `create-readme` is the README authoring packet in the `sk-doc` family. It writes current-state folder READMEs from local evidence, using packet-local templates under `assets/` and shared create-quality-control validators under `../shared`.\n11: \n12: ---\n13: \n14: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n15: \n16: ### Activation Triggers\n17: \n18: Use this packet when the request involves:\n19: \n20: - Creating or refreshing a folder `README.md`.\n21: - Documenting a project, skill, feature, component or source-code directory.\n22: - Writing developer orientation for a code folder, including topology, boundaries, entrypoints and validation.\n23: - Running `/create:readme`.\n24: \n25: Keyword triggers: `create readme`, `/create:readme`, `readme`, `folder readme`, `write readme`, `README.md`, `front-page overview`, `code folder readme`.\n26: \n27: ### When NOT to Use\n28: \n29: Use another `sk-doc` packet when:\n30: \n31: - The user wants to audit, validate, score, or optimize an existing README or another existing document without authoring or refreshing it. Use `create-quality-control`.\n32: - The user wants to scaffold a skill, agent, command, benchmark package, feature catalog, testing playbook, flowchart, or changelog. Use `create-skill`, `create-agent`, `create-command`, `create-benchmark`, `create-feature-catalog`, `create-manual-testing-playbook`, `create-flowchart`, or `create-changelog`.\n33: - The target is not markdown.\n34: - The folder is self-explanatory and a parent README or inline comments already give enough orientation.\n35: \n36: This is a nested workflow packet under the `sk-doc` parent hub. It owns README authoring only. It does not carry `graph-metadata.json`; advisor identity, skill graph metadata and cross-packet routing live at the `sk-doc` hub root.\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n41: \n42: `sk-doc` routes README authoring here: the request names a README, a folder readme, a code-folder readme or `/create:readme`.\n43: The hub's mode signal selects this packet.\n44: Three artifacts route here: a general README, a code-folder README and a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n45: The artifact decides the template before the folder decides the sections.\n46: \n47: Authoring starts from the matching template: `assets/readme-template.md` for a general README, `assets/readme-code-template.md` for a source-code folder, and `assets/architecture-template.md` for a skill package's `ARCHITECTURE.md`.\n48: `references/README.md` is the fallback route map when the artifact type or folder purpose is unclear.\n49: \n50: When nothing matches, report `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK` with the router's disambiguation checklist instead of guessing.\n51: \n52: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n53: \n54: ---\n55: \n56: ## 3. HOW IT WORKS\n57: \n58: Every run follows one operating model, whatever the artifact:\n59: \n60: 1. Read local evidence first: the target folder, nearby docs, package files, config files and existing commands. Never document unconfirmed files, commands, APIs or metrics.\n61: 2. Route by artifact type and folder purpose (`ROUTER.md` Section 3) to pick the output and template.\n62: 3. Draft current-state content only, in the smallest useful shape: a general README (Section 5) or a code-folder README (Section 6).\n63: 4. Copy the matching template as a scaffold, remove unused sections and put orientation first.\n64: 5. Validate the authored markdown and resolve local links before delivery (Section 7).\n65: \n66: Both README shapes share this lifecycle. The sections below give the detailed steps for each.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 4. README AUTHORING WORKFLOW\n71: \n72: 1. Identify the target folder, audience and README type before drafting.\n73: 2. Read the target folder contents, nearby documentation, package files, config files and existing commands.\n74: 3. Do not invent commands, files, APIs, metrics, features or capabilities.\n75: 4. Choose the smallest useful README shape. Use a general README for project, skill, feature and component roots. Use a code-folder README for source-directory orientation.\n76: 5. Write current-state documentation only. Use stable file paths, commands, feature names and source links instead of packet IDs, phase IDs, migration history or temporary planning labels.\n77: 6. Put orientation first: what this is, who it is for and how to use or navigate it.\n78: 7. Apply progressive disclosure: title and tagline answer \"what is this\", overview helps the reader decide relevance, quick start appears only when there is a runnable path and detailed reference comes later.\n79: 8. Use two-tier voice for project, skill and feature READMEs: plain narrative for overview and usage, precise reference tables for options, configuration and tool signatures.\n80: 9. Use technical reference voice for component and code-folder READMEs.\n[Output truncated. Continue reading with offset: 81]"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-readme/SKILL.md\",\"limit\":80}"},"time":{"start":1794589904143,"end":1794589904657}}}}
